1
00:00:00,269 --> 00:00:05,893
Basically, this entire roundtable session here is just going to be focused mainly on prompt engineering.
基本上，这整个圆桌会议将主要集中在提示工程上。

2
00:00:07,354 --> 00:00:13,437
Variety of perspectives at this table around prompting from a research side, from a consumer side, and from an enterprise side.
这里有来自研究、消费者和企业方面的多种视角。

3
00:00:13,998 --> 00:00:25,385
And I want to just get the whole wide range of opinions, because there's a lot of them, and just kind of open it up to discussion and explore what prompt engineering really is and what it's all about.
我想听听大家的广泛意见，因为有很多不同的观点，想要开放讨论，探索提示工程真正是什么以及它的意义。

4
00:00:27,226 --> 00:00:27,967
We'll just take it from there.
我们就从这里开始吧。

5
00:00:28,007 --> 00:00:29,708
So maybe we can go around the horn with intros.
那么，也许我们可以依次做个自我介绍。

6
00:00:30,549 --> 00:00:31,230
I can kick it off.
我来开始。

7
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:31,870
I'm Alex.
我是亚历克斯。

8
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:34,172
I lead developer relations here at Anthropic.
我在Anthropic负责开发者关系。

9
00:00:35,053 --> 00:00:39,536
Before that, I was kind of technically a prompt engineer at Anthropic.
在此之前，我在Anthropic实际上是一个提示工程师。

10
00:00:39,716 --> 00:00:51,186
I worked on our prompt engineering team and did a variety of roles spanning from a solutions architect type of thing to working on the research side.
我在我们的提示工程团队工作，参与了从解决方案架构师到研究侧的各种角色。

11
00:00:51,606 --> 00:00:53,708
So that, maybe I can hand it over to David.
所以，我可以把话题交给大卫。

12
00:00:53,888 --> 00:00:54,789
Heck yeah.
当然。

13
00:00:54,969 --> 00:00:55,730
My name is David Hershey.
我叫大卫·赫希。

14
00:00:57,551 --> 00:01:01,775
I work with customers mostly at Anthropic on a bunch of stuff.
我主要与Anthropic的客户合作，处理各种事务。

15
00:01:02,456 --> 00:01:13,546
Technical, I help people with fine-tuning, but also just like a lot of the generic things that make it hard to adopt language models, so prompting and just like how to build systems with language models, but spend most of my time working with customers.
在技术方面，我帮助人们进行微调，但也处理很多使得语言模型难以采用的一般性问题，例如提示和如何构建语言模型系统，但我大部分时间都在与客户合作。

16
00:01:14,565 --> 00:01:16,346
Well, I'm Amanda Askel.
我是阿曼达·阿斯克尔。

17
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:23,512
I lead one of the fine-tuning teams at Anthropic, where I guess I try to make Claude be honest and kind.
我在Anthropic领导一个微调团队，我想我努力让Claude诚实和友善。

18
00:01:23,812 --> 00:01:24,172
Yeah.
是的。

19
00:01:26,497 --> 00:01:27,298
My name is Zach Whitten.
我叫扎克·惠腾。

20
00:01:27,738 --> 00:01:29,659
I'm a prompt engineer at Anthropic.
我是在Anthropic的提示工程师。

21
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,521
Alex and I always argue about who the first one was.
亚历克斯和我总是争论谁是第一个。

22
00:01:32,541 --> 00:01:33,882
He says it's him, I say it's me.
他认为是他，我认为是我。

23
00:01:34,022 --> 00:01:34,582
Contested.
有争议。

24
00:01:35,643 --> 00:01:40,146
I used to work a lot with individual customers, kind of the same way David does now.
我过去与个别客户的合作很多，和大卫现在的方式差不多。

25
00:01:40,326 --> 00:01:51,793
And then as we brought more solutions architects to the team, I started working on things that are meant to raise the overall levels of ambient prompting
然后随着我们团队里增加了更多的解决方案架构师，我开始致力于提升整体环境提示水平的项目。

26
00:01:52,774 --> 00:01:58,757
society, I guess, like the prompt generator and like the various like educational materials that people use.
我想，比如提示生成器和人们使用的各种教育材料。

27
00:01:59,717 --> 00:01:59,977
Nice.
很好。

28
00:02:00,377 --> 00:02:00,617
Cool.
酷。

29
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:02,558
Well, thanks guys for all coming here.
感谢大家来到这里。

30
00:02:02,578 --> 00:02:08,741
I'm going to start with like a very broad question just so we have a frame going into the rest of our conversations here.
我想先问一个非常广泛的问题，以便为我们接下来的讨论奠定框架。

31
00:02:09,983 --> 00:02:10,984
What is prompt engineering?
什么是提示工程？

32
00:02:11,224 --> 00:02:13,486
Why is it engineering?
为什么叫工程？

33
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:15,328
What's prompt, really?
提示到底是什么？

34
00:02:16,029 --> 00:02:19,051
If anyone wants to kick that off, give your own perspective on it.
如果有谁想先谈谈，给出你自己的看法。

35
00:02:19,532 --> 00:02:20,593
Feel free to take the rein here.
随意接管这个话题。

36
00:02:21,313 --> 00:02:22,575
I feel like we have a prompt engineer.
我感觉我们有一个提示工程师。

37
00:02:23,235 --> 00:02:25,337
Yeah, exactly.
是的，确实如此。

38
00:02:25,377 --> 00:02:27,599
We're all prompt engineers in our own form.
我们每个人在自己的领域都是提示工程师。

39
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:28,920
One of us has a job.
我们中有一个人有工作。

40
00:02:28,961 --> 00:02:30,582
Yeah, maybe since it's in your time.
是啊，也许因为这是你的时间。

41
00:02:30,602 --> 00:02:33,405
One of us has a job, but the other three don't have jobs.
我们中有一个人有工作，但其他三个人没有工作。

42
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:48,114
I guess I feel like prompt engineering is trying to get the model to do things, trying to bring the most out of the model, trying to work with the model to get things done that you wouldn't have been able to do otherwise.
我觉得提示工程是试图让模型执行任务，尽量从模型中获取最大效益，努力与模型合作完成那些你原本无法做到的事情。

43
00:02:49,455 --> 00:02:52,296
So a lot of it is just clear communicating.
所以很大一部分就是清晰的沟通。

44
00:02:52,556 --> 00:02:57,639
I think at heart, talking to a model is a lot like talking to a person.
我认为，与模型对话在本质上就像与人对话。

45
00:02:59,099 --> 00:03:06,644
getting in there and understanding the psychology of the model, which Amanda is the world's most expert person in the world.
进入其中并理解模型的心理，而阿曼达是世界上最专业的专家。

46
00:03:06,964 --> 00:03:10,487
I'm going to keep going on you.
我将继续围绕这个话题。

47
00:03:10,627 --> 00:03:12,488
Why is engineering in the name?
为什么名字中有工程？

48
00:03:14,369 --> 00:03:17,772
I think the engineering part comes from the trial and error.
我认为工程部分来自于反复试验。

49
00:03:20,407 --> 00:03:29,491
One really nice thing about talking to a model that's not like talking to a person is you have this restart button, this giant go back to square zero where you just start from the beginning.
与人对话的一件好事是，与模型对话时你有这个重置按钮，这个巨大的返回零点的按钮，你可以从头开始。

50
00:03:29,531 --> 00:03:40,076
And what that gives you the ability to do that you don't have is a truly start from scratch and try out different things in an independent way so that you don't have interference from one to the other.
这使你能够真正从头开始，以独立的方式尝试不同的事情，这样你就不会互相干扰。

51
00:03:40,796 --> 00:03:48,700
And once you have that ability to experiment and to design different things, that's where the engineering part has the potential to come in.
一旦你具备了实验和设计不同事物的能力，这就是工程部分有潜力发挥作用的地方。

52
00:03:49,900 --> 00:04:08,872
Okay, so what you're saying is, as you're writing these prompts, you're typing in a message to Claude or in the API or whatever it is, being able to go back and forth with the model and iterate on this message and revert back to the clean slate every time, that process is the engineering part.
好吧，你所说的是，当你在写这些提示时，你是在向Claude或API输入消息，能够与模型进行反复沟通并迭代这个消息，每次都回到干净的状态，这个过程就是工程部分。

53
00:04:08,972 --> 00:04:13,155
This whole thing is prompt engineering all in one.
这一切都是提示工程的综合体现。

54
00:04:13,515 --> 00:04:15,997
There's another aspect of it too, which is like,
还有另一个方面，就是，

55
00:04:17,611 --> 00:04:20,712
integrating the prompts within your system as a whole.
将提示整合到你的系统整体中。

56
00:04:21,872 --> 00:04:25,533
And David has done a ton of work with customers integrating.
大卫在与客户的整合方面做了大量工作。

57
00:04:25,553 --> 00:04:30,375
A lot of times it's not just as simple as you write one prompt and you give it to the model and you're done.
很多时候，这并不仅仅是你写一个提示，然后交给模型就完成了。

58
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:31,575
In fact, it's anything but.
实际上，情况完全不是这样。

59
00:04:31,675 --> 00:04:32,775
It's way more complicated.
这要复杂得多。

60
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:38,657
Yeah, I mean, I kind of think of prompts as the way that you program models a little bit.
是的，我的意思是，我有点把提示看作是你编程模型的一种方式。

61
00:04:38,837 --> 00:04:45,159
That makes it too complicated, because I think Zach is generally right that it's just talking clearly is the most important thing.
这样说太复杂了，因为我认为扎克是对的，清晰的沟通是最重要的事情。

62
00:04:46,027 --> 00:04:51,449
But if you think about it a little bit as programming a model, you have to think about where data comes from, what data you have access to.
但如果你稍微把它想象成编程模型，你就要考虑数据来自哪里，你可以访问哪些数据。

63
00:04:51,709 --> 00:04:55,731
So if you're doing rag or something, what can I actually use and do and pass to a model?
所以如果你在做rag或者其他什么，你实际上可以使用、做和传递给模型的是什么？

64
00:04:58,072 --> 00:05:03,614
You have to think about trade-offs in latency and how much data you're providing and things like that.
你必须考虑延迟的权衡，以及你提供的数据量和其他因素。

65
00:05:03,734 --> 00:05:07,055
There's enough systems thinking that goes into how you actually build around a model.
实际上，你需要进行足够的系统思考，以便围绕模型构建。

66
00:05:07,575 --> 00:05:08,916
I think a lot of that's also the core of
我认为很多这也是核心原因，

67
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:17,382
why it like maybe is like deserves its own carve out as a thing to reason about separately from just a software engineer or a PM or something like that.
为什么它可能应该作为一个独立的课题来思考，而不是仅仅是软件工程师或PM之类的角色。

68
00:05:17,422 --> 00:05:19,603
It's like kind of its own domain of how to reason about these models.
这有点像是一个独立的领域，关于如何思考这些模型。

69
00:05:20,663 --> 00:05:24,004
Is a prompt in this sense then like natural language code?
那么在这个意义上，提示是否就像自然语言代码？

70
00:05:24,064 --> 00:05:25,804
Like is it a higher level of abstraction?
它是否是更高层次的抽象？

71
00:05:25,824 --> 00:05:27,645
Or is it kind of a separate thing?
还是说它是一种独立的东西？

72
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:35,224
I think trying to get too abstract with a prompt is a way to overcomplicate a thing.
我认为过于抽象的提示是一种使事情复杂化的方式。

73
00:05:35,684 --> 00:05:45,172
Because I think, we're going to get into it, but more often than not, the thing you want to do is just write a very clear description of a task, not try to build crazy abstractions or anything like that.
因为我认为，我们会深入讨论，但更常见的是，你想做的事情就是写一个非常清晰的任务描述，而不是尝试构建疯狂的抽象或其他类似的东西。

74
00:05:47,314 --> 00:05:54,160
But that said, you are compiling the set of instructions and things like that into outcomes a lot of times.
但话虽如此，你在很多时候是将一系列指令和其他内容编译成成果。

75
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:58,904
And so precision and a lot of the things you think about with programming, about
所以精确性和很多你在编程中考虑的事情，

76
00:05:59,764 --> 00:06:06,566
version control and managing what it looked like back then when you had this experiment and like tracking your experiments and stuff like that.
版本控制以及管理你在进行实验时的样子，以及跟踪你的实验等。

77
00:06:06,606 --> 00:06:11,608
That's all, um, you know, just equally important to code.
这些都同样重要于代码。

78
00:06:11,868 --> 00:06:20,792
So, uh, it's, it's weird to be in this paradigm where like written text, like a nice essay that you wrote is something that's looked like the same thing as code.
所以，呃，这种范式很奇怪，像是书面文本，比如你写的漂亮文章，似乎与代码是一回事。

79
00:06:21,612 --> 00:06:25,913
Uh, but it kind of is true that now we write essays and treat them like code.
呃，但现在确实是我们写文章并把它们当作代码来对待。

80
00:06:26,194 --> 00:06:27,414
And I think that's actually correct.
我认为这实际上是正确的。

81
00:06:27,890 --> 00:06:28,150
Yeah.
是的。

82
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:28,791
Okay.
好吧。

83
00:06:29,071 --> 00:06:29,571
Interesting.
有趣。

84
00:06:29,971 --> 00:06:35,475
So maybe piggybacking off of that, um, we've kind of loosely defined what prompt engineering is.
所以，也许在此基础上，我们可以松散地定义一下什么是提示工程。

85
00:06:36,155 --> 00:06:38,517
So what makes a good prompt engineer?
那么，什么样的人是好的提示工程师？

86
00:06:38,657 --> 00:06:44,621
Maybe Amanda, I'll, I'll go to you for this since you know, you're trying to hire prompt engineers more so in a research setting.
也许阿曼达，我会问你这个问题，因为你试图在研究环境中招聘提示工程师。

87
00:06:45,802 --> 00:06:46,402
What does that look like?
这看起来是什么样子的？

88
00:06:46,422 --> 00:06:47,843
What are you looking for in that type of person?
你在寻找什么样的人？

89
00:06:49,082 --> 00:06:49,943
Yeah, good question.
是的，好的问题。

90
00:06:50,544 --> 00:06:54,908
I think it's a mix of, like Zach said, sort of like clear communication.
我认为这是一种混合，就像扎克所说的，清晰的沟通。

91
00:06:55,549 --> 00:07:02,937
So the ability to just like clearly state things, clearly understand tasks, think about and describe concepts really well.
所以能够清晰地陈述事情，清晰地理解任务，思考和描述概念的能力，这就是写作的组成部分，我认为。

92
00:07:03,518 --> 00:07:05,379
That's like the kind of writing component, I think.
我实际上认为，

93
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:07,081
I actually think that
成为一个好的写作者与成为一个好的提示工程师之间的相关性并不像人们想的那样。

94
00:07:07,802 --> 00:07:12,924
being a good writer is not as correlated with being a good prompt engineer as people might think.
所以我想我和人们讨论过这个问题，因为我认为有一些论点是，也许你根本不应该在这个名字中包含工程师。

95
00:07:13,884 --> 00:07:19,447
So I guess I've had this discussion with people because I think there's some argument is like, maybe you just shouldn't have the name engineer in there.
像，为什么不直接叫作写作者？

96
00:07:19,507 --> 00:07:21,027
Like, why isn't it just like writer?
我曾经对这个更有同情心。

97
00:07:21,047 --> 00:07:23,808
I used to be more sympathetic to that.
然后我现在想，

98
00:07:24,128 --> 00:07:25,269
And then I think now I'm like,
你实际上在做的事情，人们认为你是在写一个东西，然后就完成了。

99
00:07:26,624 --> 00:07:30,266
what you're actually doing, like people think that you're writing like one thing and you're kind of like done.
然后我会说，你知道，要得到一个半不错的提示，当我坐下来与

100
00:07:31,167 --> 00:07:42,173
And then I'll be like, you know, to get like a semi-decent prompt, like when I sit down with the model, I'll like, you know, like earlier I was like prompting the model and I was just like in a 15 minute span, I'll be sending like hundreds of prompts to the model.
[Translation missing]

101
00:07:42,553 --> 00:07:44,574
It's just back and forth, back and forth, back and forth.
这就是反复的过程，来回往复。

102
00:07:45,055 --> 00:07:46,556
And so I think it's this like willingness to like
所以我认为这是对

103
00:07:47,316 --> 00:07:55,164
iterate and to look and think, what is it that was misinterpreted here, if anything, and then fix that thing.
迭代的愿望，以及思考究竟是什么地方被误解了（如果有的话），然后修正那个问题。

104
00:07:55,785 --> 00:07:57,246
So that ability to iterate.
因此，迭代的能力至关重要。

105
00:07:57,367 --> 00:08:01,351
So I'd say clear communication, that ability to iterate.
所以我会说清晰的沟通能力和这种迭代能力。

106
00:08:01,731 --> 00:08:03,933
I think also thinking about ways in which
我认为还要考虑

107
00:08:04,574 --> 00:08:05,615
your prompt might go wrong.
你的提示可能出错的地方。

108
00:08:05,855 --> 00:08:15,483
So if you have a prompt that you're going to be applying to, say, 400 cases, it's really easy to think about the typical case that it's going to be applied to, to see that it gets the right solution in that case, and then to move on.
如果你有一个要应用于400个案例的提示，很容易只考虑它将应用于的典型案例，看到在那种情况下得到正确的解决方案，然后继续前进。

109
00:08:15,563 --> 00:08:18,585
I think this is a very classic mistake that people made.
我认为这是人们常犯的经典错误。

110
00:08:19,346 --> 00:08:23,169
What you actually want to do is find the cases where it's unusual.
你真正想要做的是找到那些不寻常的案例。

111
00:08:23,489 --> 00:08:28,113
So you have to think about your prompt and be like, what are the cases where it'd be really unclear to me what I should do in this case?
所以你必须考虑你的提示，想想在那些情况下我该如何处理会非常不清楚？

112
00:08:28,533 --> 00:08:31,255
So for example, you have a prompt that says, I'm going to send you a bunch of data.
比如说，你有一个提示说，我要发送一堆数据给你。

113
00:08:31,315 --> 00:08:41,023
I want you to extract all of the rows where someone's name is, I don't know, starts with the letter J. And then you're like, well, I'm going to send it a data set where there is no such thing.
我希望你提取出所有姓名以字母J开头的行。但你可能会想，我要发送一个没有这种情况的数据集。

114
00:08:41,343 --> 00:08:45,346
There is no such name that starts with the letter J. I'm going to send it something that's not a data set.
没有以字母J开头的名字。我可能会发送一些不是数据集的东西。

115
00:08:45,946 --> 00:08:47,468
I might also just send it an empty string.
我也可能只发送一个空字符串。

116
00:08:48,008 --> 00:08:51,491
These are all of the cases you have to try, because then you're like, what does it do in these cases?
这些都是你必须尝试的情况，因为那样你就能知道它在这些情况下会如何反应。

117
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:55,454
And then you can give it more instructions for how it should deal with that case.
然后你可以给它更多的指示，告诉它该如何处理这种情况。

118
00:08:55,814 --> 00:09:04,700
I work with customers so often where, like, you're an engineer, you're building something, and there's a part in your prompt where a customer of theirs is going to write something.
我经常和客户合作，通常你是一名工程师，你正在构建一些东西，而在你的提示中有一部分是他们的客户将要填写的内容。

119
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:05,140
Yeah.
是的。

120
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:09,763
And they all think about, like, these really perfectly phrased things that they think someone's going to type into their chatbot.
他们总是想着这些非常完美的措辞，认为用户会在他们的聊天机器人中输入。

121
00:09:10,163 --> 00:09:14,907
In reality, it's like they never used the shift key and, like, every other word is a typo.
实际上，他们似乎从来没有用过大写键，每两个字就有一个错字。

122
00:09:15,027 --> 00:09:15,567
They think it's Google.
他们认为这是谷歌。

123
00:09:15,587 --> 00:09:16,708
And there's no punctuation.
而且没有标点符号。

124
00:09:16,728 --> 00:09:16,808
Yeah.
是的。

125
00:09:17,068 --> 00:09:20,150
They just put in, like, random words with no question.
他们输入的只是随机的词语，没有提出问题。

126
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:30,739
So you have these evals that are like these beautifully structured what their users ideally would type in, but like being able to go the next step to reason about like what your actual traffic is going to be like, what people are actually going to try to do.
所以你会发现这些评估就像用户理想中会输入的结构化内容，但能够进一步推理出你的实际流量将会是什么样子，以及人们实际上会尝试做什么。

127
00:09:30,759 --> 00:09:32,621
That's a different level of thinking kind of.
这是一种不同层面的思考。

128
00:09:33,621 --> 00:09:36,623
One thing you said that really resonated with me is reading the model responses.
你提到的一个让我深有感触的事情是要阅读模型的响应。

129
00:09:37,783 --> 00:09:41,445
In a machine learning context, you're supposed to look at the data.
在机器学习的背景下，应该查看数据。

130
00:09:41,505 --> 00:09:42,506
It's almost a cliche.
这几乎成了一个陈词滥调。

131
00:09:42,746 --> 00:09:43,366
Look at your data.
查看你的数据。

132
00:09:43,446 --> 00:09:47,908
And I feel like the equivalent for prompting is look at the model outputs.
而我觉得提示的等价物是查看模型的输出。

133
00:09:48,108 --> 00:09:50,530
Just reading a lot of outputs and reading them closely.
就是要多读输出，并仔细阅读。

134
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:52,451
Dave and I were talking on the way here.
大卫和我在来这里的路上聊天时提到过。

135
00:09:52,771 --> 00:09:56,353
One thing that people will do is they'll think step by step in their prompt.
有些人会在他们的提示中逐步思考。

136
00:09:57,453 --> 00:10:09,464
And they won't check to make sure that the model is actually thinking step-by-step because the model might take it in a more abstract or general sense rather than like, no, literally you have to write down your thoughts in these specific tags.
但他们不会检查以确保模型确实在逐步思考，因为模型可能会以更抽象或一般的方式理解，而不是说，不，字面上你必须用这些特定的标签写下你的想法。

137
00:10:10,705 --> 00:10:16,210
So yeah, if you aren't reading the model outputs, you might not even notice that it's making that mistake.
所以是的，如果你不阅读模型的输出，你可能甚至不会注意到它在犯那个错误。

138
00:10:16,991 --> 00:10:18,331
Yeah, that's interesting.
是的，这很有趣。

139
00:10:19,832 --> 00:10:27,213
There is that kind of weird theory of mind piece to being a prompt engineer where you have to think almost about how the model is going to view your instructions.
作为一名提示工程师，确实有一种奇怪的心智理论部分，你几乎必须考虑模型将如何看待你的指示。

140
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:36,395
But then if you're writing for like an enterprise use case too, you have to think about how the user is going to talk to the model as like you're the third party sitting there in that weird relationship.
但如果你是为企业用例写作，你也必须考虑用户将如何与模型交流，就像你是那个在奇怪关系中的第三方。

141
00:10:37,915 --> 00:10:38,495
Yeah.
是的。

142
00:10:39,775 --> 00:10:41,296
On the theory of mind piece, one thing I would say is
关于心智理论部分，我想说的是

143
00:10:43,979 --> 00:10:47,582
It's so hard to write instructions down for a task.
为一个任务写下指令是如此困难。

144
00:10:48,482 --> 00:10:55,828
It's so hard to untangle in your own brain all of the stuff that you know that Quad does not know and write it down.
在你自己的大脑中理清所有你知道的，但Quad不知道的东西并将其写下来是如此艰难。

145
00:10:56,048 --> 00:11:05,475
It's just an immensely challenging thing to strip away all of the assumptions you have and be able to very clearly communicate the full fact set of information that is needed to a model.
这真的是一项极具挑战性的任务，需要剥离掉所有的假设，并能够非常清晰地向模型传达所需的完整信息集。

146
00:11:05,895 --> 00:11:09,098
I think that's another thing that really differentiates a good prompt engineer from a bad one.
我认为这也是区分优秀提示工程师和差劲者的另一方面。

147
00:11:10,347 --> 00:11:20,840
If you, a lot of people will sort of like just write down the things they know, but they don't really take the time to systematically break out what is the actual full set of information you need to know to understand this task.
许多人只是简单地写下他们知道的内容，但他们并没有真正花时间系统地分析理解这个任务所需的完整信息集。

148
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:21,521
Right.
对。

149
00:11:21,821 --> 00:11:26,407
And that's kind of like a very clear thing I see a lot is prompts where it's just like
这就像我看到的非常明显的事情是，提示只是

150
00:11:27,682 --> 00:11:28,322
is conditioned.
是有条件的。

151
00:11:28,342 --> 00:11:35,888
The prompt that someone wrote is so conditioned on their prior understanding of a task that when they show it to me, I'm like, this makes no sense.
有人写的提示依赖于他们对任务的先前理解，以至于当他们把它展示给我时，我会觉得这毫无意义。

152
00:11:36,168 --> 00:11:41,291
None of the words you wrote make any sense because I don't know anything about your interesting use case.
你写的那些词毫无意义，因为我对你的有趣用例一无所知。

153
00:11:42,132 --> 00:11:48,996
But I think a good way to think about prompt engineering in that front and a good skill for it is just can you actually
但我认为，考虑提示工程的一个好方法和重要技能就是，你是否能够真正

154
00:11:50,117 --> 00:11:57,928
step back from what you know and communicate to this weird system that knows a lot but not everything about what it needs to know to do a task.
从你所知道的事情中退一步，向这个了解很多但并不完全了解完成任务所需内容的奇怪系统进行沟通。

155
00:11:58,533 --> 00:12:04,015
Yeah, the amount of times I've seen someone's prompt and then being like, I can't do the task based on this prompt.
是的，我看到某个人的提示后，常常会想，我无法根据这个提示完成任务。

156
00:12:04,075 --> 00:12:10,698
And like, I'm human level and you're giving this to something that is worse than me and expecting it to do better.
而且，我是人类水平，而你却把这个交给一个比我差的东西，并期望它做得更好。

157
00:12:10,758 --> 00:12:11,719
And I'm like, yeah.
我会说，是的。

158
00:12:12,819 --> 00:12:23,304
Yeah, there is that interesting thing with like, I mean, current, you know, current models don't really do a good job of asking good probing questions in response, like a human would.
是的，确实存在这样有趣的现象，目前的模型在回应时并没有很好地提出好的探询问题，就像人类一样。

159
00:12:23,905 --> 00:12:27,970
If I'm giving Zach directions on how to do something, he'll be like, this doesn't make any sense.
如果我给扎克指示如何做某事，他会说，这毫无意义。

160
00:12:28,190 --> 00:12:30,173
What am I supposed to do at this step or here and here?
我在这个步骤或这里和这里应该做什么？

161
00:12:30,834 --> 00:12:31,815
Model doesn't do that, right?
模型并不会这样做，对吧？

162
00:12:32,175 --> 00:12:39,425
So you have to, as yourself, think through what that other person would say and then go back to your prompt and answer those questions.
所以你必须作为自己去思考那个其他人会怎么说，然后回到你的提示中回答那些问题。

163
00:12:40,806 --> 00:12:44,247
You could ask it to do that.
你可以要求它做到这一点。

164
00:12:44,267 --> 00:12:49,989
I was going to say one of the first things I do with my initial prompt is like I'll give it the prompt and then I'll be like, I don't want you to follow these instructions.
我想说，我给出初始提示时做的第一件事就是，我会给它提示，然后我会说，我不希望你遵循这些指示。

165
00:12:50,069 --> 00:12:54,051
I just want you to tell me the ways in which they're unclear or any ambiguities or anything you don't understand.
我只希望你告诉我哪些地方不明确，或者有什么歧义，或者有什么你不理解的地方。

166
00:12:54,131 --> 00:12:56,692
And it doesn't always get it perfect, but it is interesting that
它并不总是完美，但有趣的是

167
00:12:57,632 --> 00:12:59,212
That is one thing you can do.
这是你可以做的事情之一。

168
00:12:59,312 --> 00:13:04,494
And then also sometimes if people see that the model makes a mistake, the thing that they don't often do is just ask the model.
然后，有时如果人们看到模型犯了错误，他们通常不做的事情就是问模型。

169
00:13:04,714 --> 00:13:06,454
So they say to the model, you got this wrong.
所以他们对模型说，你错了。

170
00:13:06,534 --> 00:13:07,514
Can you think about why?
你能想想为什么吗？

171
00:13:07,594 --> 00:13:10,995
And can you maybe write an edited version of my instructions that would make you not get it wrong?
你能否写一个修改过的版本的我的指示，让你不会再出错？

172
00:13:11,535 --> 00:13:14,516
And a lot of the time, the model just gets it right.
许多时候，模型就是能正确理解。

173
00:13:14,536 --> 00:13:16,016
The model's like, oh yeah, here's what was unclear.
模型会说，哦，是的，这里不清楚。

174
00:13:16,336 --> 00:13:17,997
Here's a fix to the instructions.
这里是对指示的修正。

175
00:13:18,037 --> 00:13:19,237
And then you put those in and it works.
然后你把这些放进去，它就能正常工作。

176
00:13:20,413 --> 00:13:23,094
Okay, I'm actually really curious about this personally almost.
好吧，我实际上对此非常好奇。

177
00:13:23,695 --> 00:13:24,915
Is that true that that works?
这是真的吗，这样是有效的？

178
00:13:25,035 --> 00:13:29,097
Like does the model, is the model able to spot its mistakes that way?
模型能否以这种方式发现自己的错误？

179
00:13:29,137 --> 00:13:32,239
Like when it gets something wrong, you say like, why did you get this wrong?
当它出错时，你说，比如，你为什么会错？

180
00:13:32,279 --> 00:13:37,181
And then it tells you maybe something like, okay, how could I phrase this to you in the future?
然后它告诉你，可能像这样，好吧，我将来该如何向你表达这个？

181
00:13:37,201 --> 00:13:38,101
So you get it right.
这样你就能正确理解。

182
00:13:38,702 --> 00:13:40,643
Is there an element of like truth to that?
这其中有没有某种真实的成分？

183
00:13:40,683 --> 00:13:45,665
Or is that just kind of a hallucination on the model's part around what it thinks its limits are?
还是这只是模型对自己限制的某种幻觉？

184
00:13:46,038 --> 00:13:52,143
I think if you explain to it what it got wrong, it can identify things in the query sometimes.
我认为如果你向它解释它错了什么，它有时可以识别出查询中的问题。

185
00:13:52,263 --> 00:13:53,604
I think this varies by task.
我认为这因任务而异。

186
00:13:53,624 --> 00:13:59,909
This is one of those things where I'm like, I'm not sure what percentage of the time it gets it right, but I always try it because sometimes it does.
这是我不太确定它正确的比例，但我总是尝试，因为有时它确实能做到。

187
00:13:59,929 --> 00:14:00,890
You learn something.
你会学到某些东西。

188
00:14:01,750 --> 00:14:06,955
Any time you go back to the model or back and forth with the model, you learn something about what's going on.
每次你回到模型或与模型反复交流时，你都会学到一些关于发生了什么的东西。

189
00:14:07,115 --> 00:14:09,737
I think you're giving away information if you don't at least try.
如果你不至少尝试，你是在放弃信息。

190
00:14:11,423 --> 00:14:12,164
That's interesting.
这很有趣。

191
00:14:13,144 --> 00:14:15,086
Amanda, I'm going to keep asking you a few more questions here.
阿曼达，我要继续问你几个问题。

192
00:14:16,086 --> 00:14:26,174
One thing maybe for everybody watching this is we have these Slack channels at Entropic where people can add Claude into the Slack channel, then you can talk to Claude through it.
也许对于观看这个的每个人来说，我们在Entropic有这些Slack频道，人们可以把Claude添加到Slack频道中，然后你可以通过它与Claude交谈。

193
00:14:26,534 --> 00:14:31,778
And Amanda has a Slack channel that a lot of people follow of her interactions with Claude.
阿曼达有一个Slack频道，很多人关注她与Claude的互动。

194
00:14:32,438 --> 00:14:42,763
And one thing that I see you always do in there, which you probably do the most of anyone at Anthropic, is use the model to help you in a variety of different scenarios.
我看到你在里面总是做的一件事，可能是Anthropic中做得最多的，就是利用模型帮助你处理各种不同的场景。

195
00:14:42,804 --> 00:14:46,385
I think you put a lot of trust into the model in the research setting.
我认为你在研究环境中对模型投入了很多信任。

196
00:14:47,946 --> 00:14:51,148
Curious how you've developed those intuitions for when to trust the model.
我很好奇你是如何发展出这些在何时信任模型的直觉的。

197
00:14:51,168 --> 00:14:54,890
Is that just a matter of usage, experience, or is it something else?
这只是使用、经验的问题，还是其他什么？

198
00:14:55,987 --> 00:15:00,829
I think I don't trust the model ever, and then I just hammer on it.
我觉得我从来不信任模型，然后我就不断试探它。

199
00:15:00,889 --> 00:15:06,251
So I think the reason why you see me do that a lot is that that is like me being like, can I trust you to do this task?
所以我认为你看到我这样做的原因是，这就像我在问，能否信

200
00:15:06,271 --> 00:15:15,075
Because there's some things, you know, models are kind of strange if you go slightly out of distribution, like, you know, like, you just go into areas where they haven't been trained or they're kind of unusual.
[Translation missing]

201
00:15:15,155 --> 00:15:20,797
Sometimes you're like, oh, actually, you're much less reliable here, even though it's a fairly, like, simple task.
有时候你会想，哦，实际上，在这方面它的表现远不如我所期望的，尽管这是一项相对简单的任务。

202
00:15:21,077 --> 00:15:25,479
I think that's happening less and less over time as models get better, but you want to make sure you're not in that kind of space.
我认为随着模型的不断改进，这种情况正在越来越少，但你仍然要确保自己不陷入这种境地。

203
00:15:26,079 --> 00:15:33,007
So yeah, I don't think I trust it by default, but I think in ML, people often want to look across really large data sets.
所以是的，我不认为我会默认信任它，但我觉得在机器学习中，人们往往希望查看非常大的数据集。

204
00:15:33,227 --> 00:15:35,229
And I'm like, when does it make sense to do that?
我就想，什么时候这样做才有意义？

205
00:15:35,409 --> 00:15:42,016
And I think the answer is when you get relatively low signal from each data point, you want to look across many, many data points.
我认为答案是，当你从每个数据点获取的信号相对较低时，你希望查看许多数据点。

206
00:15:42,456 --> 00:15:44,238
Because you basically want to get rid of the noise.
因为你基本上是想消除噪音。

207
00:15:44,678 --> 00:15:49,203
With a lot of prompting tasks, I think you actually get really high signal from each query.
在许多提示任务中，我认为你实际上从每个查询中获得了非常高的信号。

208
00:15:49,763 --> 00:15:58,832
And so if you have a really well-constructed set of a few hundred prompts, that I think can be much more signal than thousands that aren't as well-crafted.
所以如果你有一组构建得非常好的几百个提示，我认为这比几千个构建得不那么好的提示产生更多的信号。

209
00:15:59,252 --> 00:15:59,772
And so I think
所以我认为

210
00:16:01,027 --> 00:16:14,278
I do think I can trust the model if I look at 100 outputs of it, and it's really consistent, and I know that I've constructed those to basically figure out all of the edge cases and all of the weird things that the model might do, strange inputs, et cetera.
如果我查看它的100个输出，并且它们非常一致，我知道我构建了这些以基本上涵盖所有边缘案例和模型可能会处理的奇怪输入等等，我确实认为我可以信任模型。

211
00:16:15,118 --> 00:16:20,883
I trust that probably more than a much more loosely constructed set of several thousand.
我可能更信任这个，而不是一组构建得更松散的几千个。

212
00:16:22,039 --> 00:16:27,266
I think in ML, a lot of times the signals are numbers.
我认为在机器学习中，很多时候信号是数字。

213
00:16:29,209 --> 00:16:30,731
Did you predict this thing right or not?
你是否正确预测了这个事情？

214
00:16:31,512 --> 00:16:37,781
And it'd be like looking at the log probs of a model and trying to intuit things, which you can do, but it's kind of sketchy.
这就像查看模型的对数概率并试图直观理解一些东西，虽然你可以这样做，但这有点不靠谱。

215
00:16:39,949 --> 00:16:49,957
I feel like the fact that models output, more often than not, a lot of stuff, like words and things, there's just fundamentally so much to learn between the lines of what it's writing and why and how.
我觉得模型输出的事实，往往是大量的信息，比如单词和其他东西，根本上有太多东西可以在它所写的内容之间学习，以及原因和方式。

216
00:16:50,197 --> 00:16:51,578
And that's part of what it is.
这就是它的部分内容。

217
00:16:52,099 --> 00:16:54,261
It's not just, did it get the task right or not?
这不仅仅是，它是否完成了任务。

218
00:16:54,941 --> 00:16:57,023
It's like, how did it get there?
而是，它是如何到达这个结果的？

219
00:16:57,043 --> 00:16:58,324
How was it thinking about it?
它是如何思考这个问题的？

220
00:16:58,384 --> 00:16:59,104
What steps did it go through?
它经历了哪些步骤？

221
00:16:59,144 --> 00:17:01,426
You learn a lot about what is going on.
你可以了解到很多事情。

222
00:17:01,446 --> 00:17:03,808
Or at least you can try to get a better sense, I think.
或者至少你可以尝试更好地理解，我认为。

223
00:17:04,128 --> 00:17:08,852
But that's where a lot of information comes from to me, is by reading the details of what came out, not just the result.
但对我来说，很多信息来自于阅读输出的细节，而不仅仅是结果。

224
00:17:09,873 --> 00:17:18,140
I think also the very best of prompting can kind of make the difference between a failed and a successful experiment.
我认为，最好的提示实际上可以在失败和成功的实验之间产生差异。

225
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:29,008
So sometimes I can get annoyed if people don't focus enough on the prompting component of their experiment because I'm like, this can in fact be like the difference between like 1% performance in the model or 0.1%.
所以有时候，如果人们不够专注于他们实验的提示组件，我可能会感到恼火，因为我觉得，这实际上可以是模型性能在1%和0.1%之间的差异。

226
00:17:31,610 --> 00:17:38,858
in such a way that your experiment doesn't succeed if it's at top 5% model performance, but it does succeed if it's at top 1% or top 0.1%.
这样一来，如果你的实验在前5%的模型性能上不成功，但如果在前1%或前0.1%上就成功了。

227
00:17:39,619 --> 00:17:48,689
And then I'm like, if you're going to spend time over like coding your experiment really nicely, but then just like not spend time on the prompt, that doesn't, I don't know, that doesn't make sense to me.
然后我想，如果你要花时间好好编码你的实验，但却没有花时间在提示上，这对我来说没有意义。

228
00:17:48,709 --> 00:17:51,732
Cause I'm like, that can be the difference between life and death of your experiment.
因为我觉得，这可能是你实验生死之间的差异。

229
00:17:51,972 --> 00:17:52,212
Yeah.
是的。

230
00:17:52,492 --> 00:17:53,473
And with the deployment too.
而且在部署上也是如此。

231
00:17:53,693 --> 00:17:54,213
Yeah.
是的。

232
00:17:54,273 --> 00:17:56,674
It's so easy to, oh, we can't ship this.
这很容易，哦，我们不能发布这个。

233
00:17:56,894 --> 00:18:00,275
And then you change the prompt around and suddenly it's working.
然后你改变提示，突然之间它就有效了。

234
