这在你实际到达提示时过度应用了，因为你所说的，到他们经历了所有这些其他东西时，实际上并不是正在建模的内容。

394
00:31:05,117 --> 00:31:17,709
Yeah, the first thing that I feel like you should try is, I mean, I used to give people this thought experiment where it's like, imagine you have this task, you've hired a temp agency to send someone to do this task.
是的，我觉得你应该尝试的第一件事是，我的意思是，我曾经给人们这个思维实验，想象一下你有这个任务，你雇佣了一个临时机构来派人完成这个任务。

395
00:31:18,070 --> 00:31:25,056
This person arrives, you know, they're pretty competent, they know a lot about your industry and so forth, but they don't know like the name of your company.
这个人到达时，你知道，他们相当能干，他们对你的行业了解很多等等，但他们不知道你公司的名字。

396
00:31:25,097 --> 00:31:28,960
They've literally just shown up and they're like, hey, I was told you guys had a job for me to do.
他们字面上刚到，像是，嘿，我被告知你们有一份工作让我做。

397
00:31:29,821 --> 00:31:30,462
tell me about it.
告诉我关于它的事情。

398
00:31:30,642 --> 00:31:32,403
And then it's like, what would you say to that person?
然后你会对那个人说什么？

399
00:31:33,103 --> 00:31:34,484
And you may use these metaphors.
你可能会使用这些隐喻。

400
00:31:34,504 --> 00:31:40,829
You may say things like, we want you to detect good charts.
你可能会说

401
00:31:41,670 --> 00:31:44,171
What we mean by a good chart here isn't it doesn't need to be perfect.
我们所说的一个好的图表并不一定要完美。

402
00:31:44,191 --> 00:31:47,093
You don't need to go look up whether all of the details are correct.
你不需要去查找所有细节是否正确。

403
00:31:47,133 --> 00:31:50,656
It just needs to have its axes labeled.
它只需要标注好坐标轴。

404
00:31:50,816 --> 00:31:54,899
And so think about maybe high school level, good chart.
所以可以考虑一下高中水平的好图表。

405
00:31:55,339 --> 00:31:56,941
You may say exactly that to that person.
你可以直接对那个人说这句话。

406
00:31:56,961 --> 00:31:58,722
And you're not saying to them, you are a high school
你并不是在说，你是一个高中生。

407
00:31:59,102 --> 00:32:05,710
You wouldn't say that to them, you'd be like, you're a high school teacher reading charts.
你不会那样说，你会说，你是一个高中老师在阅读图表。

408
00:32:07,052 --> 00:32:16,182
Yeah, so sometimes I'm just like, yeah, it's like, like the whole, like, if I read it, I'm just like, yeah, imagine this person who just has very little context, but they're quite competent, they understand a lot of things about the world.
是的，有时候我只是这样想，如果我在阅读它，我就会想，想象这个人几乎没有背景知识，但他们很有能力，对世界的很多事情都有理解。

409
00:32:16,883 --> 00:32:19,966
try the first version that actually assumes that they might know things about the world.
尝试第一个版本，假设他们可能对世界有一些了解。

410
00:32:20,107 --> 00:32:22,469
And if that doesn't work, you can maybe do tweaks and stuff.
如果这不奏效，你可以做一些调整。

411
00:32:22,489 --> 00:32:24,792
But so often, the first thing I try is that.
但我尝试的第一件事通常就是这个。

412
00:32:24,812 --> 00:32:26,514
And then I'm like, that just worked.
然后我想，这就是有效的。

413
00:32:26,954 --> 00:32:31,279
And then people are like, oh, I didn't think to just tell it all about myself and all about the task I want to do.
然后人们会说，哦，我没想到要告诉它我自己和我想做的任务的所有事情。

414
00:32:31,499 --> 00:32:35,423
I've carried this thing that Alex told me to so many customers where it's like,
我把亚历克斯告诉我的这个方法带给了很多客户，像是，

415
00:32:36,004 --> 00:32:37,004
They're like, oh, my prompt doesn't work.
他们会说，哦，我的提示不起作用。

416
00:32:37,044 --> 00:32:37,745
Can you help me fix it?
你能帮我修正一下吗？

417
00:32:37,765 --> 00:32:40,366
And I'm like, well, can you describe to me what the task was?
我会问，好吧，你能告诉我任务是什么吗？

418
00:32:40,426 --> 00:32:46,648
And I'm like, OK, now what you just said to me, just voice record that, and then transcribe it, and then paste it into the prompt.
然后我说，好，现在你刚才跟我说的，录音，然后转录，把它粘贴到提示中。

419
00:32:47,268 --> 00:32:50,010
And it's a better prompt than what you wrote.
这比你写的提示要好。

420
00:32:50,170 --> 00:32:53,211
This is like a laziness shortcut, I think, to some extent.
我觉得这在某种程度上是懒惰的捷径。

421
00:32:53,611 --> 00:32:56,892
People write something that they, I just think people, I'm lazy.
人们写一些他们，我只是觉得人们，我很懒。

422
00:32:56,912 --> 00:32:57,713
A lot of people are lazy.
很多人都很懒。

423
00:32:57,793 --> 00:33:00,614
We had that in prompt assistance the other day, where somebody was like,
前几天我们在提示帮助中碰到这种情况，有人说，

424
00:33:01,494 --> 00:33:05,076
here's the thing, here's what I want it to do and here's what it's actually doing instead.
这是我想要的功能，这里是它实际上正在做的事情。

425
00:33:05,176 --> 00:33:11,039
So then I just literally copied the thing that they said I wanted to do and pasted it in.
所以我就简单地复制了他们说的我想要做的事情，然后粘贴进去。

426
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:17,663
Yeah, I think a lot of people still haven't quite wrapped their heads around what they're really doing when they're prompting.
是的，我觉得很多人仍然没有完全理解他们在提示时真正做的是什么。

427
00:33:17,703 --> 00:33:20,905
Like a lot of people see a text box and they think it's like a Google search box.
很多人看到一个文本框，他们认为它就像是谷歌搜索框。

428
00:33:21,325 --> 00:33:23,906
They type in keywords and maybe that's more on like the chat side.
他们输入关键字，这可能更多是在聊天方面。

429
00:33:24,386 --> 00:33:24,947
But then on like
但在企业方面，

430
00:33:25,427 --> 00:33:37,617
The enterprise side of things, you're writing a prompt for an application, there is still this weird thing to it where people are trying to take all these little shortcuts in their prompt and just thinking that this line carries a lot of weight.
你为一个应用程序写提示，仍然有这种奇怪的事情，人们试图在他们的提示中采取所有这些小捷径，只是认为这一行承载了很多分量。

431
00:33:37,657 --> 00:33:42,781
Yeah, I think you obsess over getting the perfect little line of information and instruction as opposed to...
是的，我觉得你过于执着于获取完美的信息和指令，而不是...

432
00:33:43,502 --> 00:33:47,725
how you just described that graph thing is like, I would be a dream if I read prompts like that.
你刚才描述的图表的方式就像，我会梦想阅读这样的提示。

433
00:33:48,586 --> 00:33:52,409
If someone's like, well, you do this and this, and there's some stuff to consider about this and all that.
如果有人说，好吧，你这样做，那样做，还有一些需要考虑的事情。

434
00:33:52,789 --> 00:33:54,070
But that's just not how people write prompts.
但这不是人们写提示的方式。

435
00:33:54,090 --> 00:34:01,596
They work so hard to find the perfect, insightful, like a perfect graph looks exactly like this exact perfect thing.
他们努力寻找完美、深刻的，就像一个完美的图表看起来完全是这个完美的东西。

436
00:34:03,718 --> 00:34:04,339
You can't do that.
你不能那样做。

437
00:34:04,399 --> 00:34:13,495
It's just very hard to ever write that set of instructions down prescriptively as opposed to how we actually talk to humans about it, which is like try to instill some amount of the intuitions you have.
实际上，以处方方式写下那组指令是非常困难的，而不是我们与人类谈论时的方式，那就是尽量灌输你拥有的一些直觉。

438
00:34:14,025 --> 00:34:15,126
We also give them outs.
我们也给他们留有余地。

439
00:34:15,266 --> 00:34:17,907
This is a thing that people can often forget in prompts.
这是人们在提示中经常会忘记的事情。

440
00:34:18,167 --> 00:34:21,549
I'm like, so cases, if there's an edge case, think about what you want the model to do.
我在想，如果有边缘案例，考虑一下你希望模型做什么。

441
00:34:21,570 --> 00:34:26,172
Because by default, it will try the best to follow your instructions, much as the person from the temp agency would.
因为默认情况下，它会尽力遵循你的指令，就像临时机构的人会做的那样。

442
00:34:26,592 --> 00:34:30,095
Because they're like, well, they didn't tell me how to get in touch with anyone if I have no idea.
因为他们会想，好吧，他们没有告诉我如果我不知道该如何与任何人联系。

443
00:34:30,115 --> 00:34:33,316
If I'm just given a picture of a goat, and I'm like, what do I do?
如果我只是一张山羊的照片，我会想，我该怎么办？

444
00:34:33,877 --> 00:34:35,678
This isn't even a chart.
这甚至不是一个图表。

445
00:34:35,918 --> 00:34:38,319
How good is a picture of a goat as a chart?
一张山羊的照片作为图表好到什么程度？

446
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:39,240
I just don't know.
我真的不知道。

447
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:42,342
And if you instead say something like, if,
如果你改为说，如果，

448
00:34:42,822 --> 00:34:47,566
something weird happens, and you're really not sure what to do, just output in tags, unsure.
发生了奇怪的事情，而你真的不确定该怎么办，就输出标签，不确定。

449
00:34:47,586 --> 00:34:53,230
And then you can go look through the unsures that you got and be like, OK, cool, it didn't do anything weird.
然后你可以查看你得到的不确定项，想，好的，没做什么奇怪的事情。

450
00:34:53,290 --> 00:34:57,994
Whereas by default, if you don't give the person the option, they're like, it's a good chart.
而默认情况下，如果你不给那个人选择，他们会说，这是一个好的图表。

451
00:34:58,614 --> 00:35:00,516
And then people will be like, how do I do that?
然后人们会问，我该怎么做？

452
00:35:00,536 --> 00:35:02,277
And then you're like, well, give it an out.
然后你就会说，嗯，给它一个备选方案。

453
00:35:02,357 --> 00:35:05,320
Give it something to do if it's a really unexpected input happens.
如果发生了非常意外的输入，就给它一些事情去做。

454
00:35:05,640 --> 00:35:10,384
And then you also improved your data quality by doing that, too, because you found all the screwed up examples.
通过这样做，你也提高了数据质量，因为你发现了所有那些错误的例子。

455
00:35:10,504 --> 00:35:10,764
Oh, yeah.
哦，是的。

456
00:35:11,633 --> 00:35:20,560
It's my favorite thing about iterating on tests with Claude is the most common outcome is I find all of the terrible tests I accidentally wrote because like it gets it wrong.
我最喜欢与Claude迭代测试的事情是，最常见的结果是我发现了所有我意外写下的糟糕测试，因为它搞错了。

457
00:35:20,580 --> 00:35:21,461
I'm like, oh, why did it get wrong?
我心想，哦，为什么它会出错？

458
00:35:21,481 --> 00:35:22,602
I was like, oh, I was wrong.
我意识到，哦，我错了。

459
00:35:22,722 --> 00:35:23,423
Yeah.
是的。

460
00:35:23,543 --> 00:35:24,403
Yeah.
是的。

461
00:35:24,784 --> 00:35:25,244
Yeah.
是的。

462
00:35:25,704 --> 00:35:29,788
If I was like a company working with this, I do think I would just give my prompts to people.
如果我是一家与此合作的公司，我确实认为我会把我的提示给别人。

463
00:35:31,189 --> 00:35:36,254
