00:46:02,710 --> 00:46:08,414
They're very similar in the sense that the level of care and nuance that's put into them.
在某种意义上它们非常相似，因为其中所投入的关心和细微之处。

589
00:46:08,535 --> 00:46:14,279
I think for research, you're looking for variety and diversity a lot more.
我认为在研究中，你更看重多样性和丰富性。

590
00:46:15,520 --> 00:46:24,106
If I could boil it down to one thing, I've noticed Amanda's not the biggest fan of having lots of example, or one or two examples.
如果我能将其归结为一件事，我注意到阿曼达并不是特别喜欢有很多例子，或者一两个例子。

591
00:46:24,146 --> 00:46:26,548
Too few, because the model will latch onto those.
太少了，因为模型会依附于这些。

592
00:46:27,820 --> 00:46:33,269
And in prompts that I might write or that I've seen David write, we have a lot of examples.
而在我可能写的提示或我看到David写的提示中，我们有很多例子。

593
00:46:33,829 --> 00:46:37,975
I like to just go crazy and add examples until I'm good.
我喜欢疯狂地添加例子，直到我满意。

594
00:46:38,763 --> 00:46:41,104
I feel like I'm about to drop dead because I've added so many of them.
我感觉快要累死了，因为我添加了太多。

595
00:46:43,266 --> 00:46:50,250
And I think that's because when you're in a consumer application, you really value reliability.
我认为这是因为在消费者应用中，你非常看重可靠性。

596
00:46:51,031 --> 00:46:52,952
You care a ton about the format.
你非常在意格式。

597
00:46:53,832 --> 00:46:56,814
And it's sort of fine if all the answers are the same.
如果所有答案都是一样的，那也算可以。

598
00:46:56,874 --> 00:47:00,076
In fact, you almost want them to be the same in a lot of ways.
实际上，在很多方面你几乎希望它们是一样的。

599
00:47:00,436 --> 00:47:01,097
Not necessarily.
不一定。

600
00:47:01,137 --> 00:47:04,039
You want to be responsive to the user's desires.
你希望对用户的需求做出响应。

601
00:47:05,148 --> 00:47:16,250
Whereas a lot of times when you're prompting for research, you're trying to really tap into the range of possibilities that the model can explore.
而在很多情况下，当你为研究进行提示时，你实际上是在努力挖掘模型可以探索的可能性范围。

602
00:47:16,410 --> 00:47:20,731
And by having some examples, you're actually constraining that a little bit.
而通过提供一些示例，你实际上是稍微限制了这一点。

603
00:47:20,851 --> 00:47:28,932
So I guess just on a how the prompts look level, that's probably the biggest difference I notice, is how many examples are in the prompt.
所以我想，在提示的外观层面上，我注意到的最大区别可能是提示中包含了多少示例。

604
00:47:28,972 --> 00:47:34,573
Which is not to say that I've never seen you write a prompt with examples, but does that ring true for you?
这并不是说我从未见过你写带有示例的提示，但这对你来说是否真实？

605
00:47:35,089 --> 00:47:41,911
Yeah, I think when I give examples, often I actually try and make the examples not like the data that the model is going to see.
是的，我认为当我给出示例时，我通常会尝试使这些示例与模型将要看到的数据不太相似。

606
00:47:42,351 --> 00:47:55,975
So they're intentionally illustrative because if I give it examples that are very like the data it's going to see, I just think it is going to give me a really consistent response that might not actually be what I want.
所以它们是故意具有说明性的，因为如果我给出与模型将要看到的数据非常相似的示例，我只会认为它会给我一个非常一致的响应，而这可能并不是我想要的。

607
00:47:56,796 --> 00:48:03,281
Because my data that I'm running it on might be extremely varied, and so I don't want it to just try and give me this really rote output.
因为我正在运行的数据可能非常多样化，所以我不希望它只是试图给我这种非常机械的输出。

608
00:48:03,381 --> 00:48:05,183
Often I want it to be much more responsive.
我通常希望它能够更加响应。

609
00:48:05,543 --> 00:48:13,029
It's kind of like much more cognitive tasks, essentially, where I'm like, you have to see this sample and really think about, in this sample, what is the right answer?
这就像是更认知的任务，基本上，我想，你必须查看这个样本，并认真思考，在这个样本中，什么是正确的答案？

610
00:48:13,049 --> 00:48:19,335
And so that means that sometimes I'll actually take examples that are just very distinct from the ones that I'm going to be running it on.
所以这意味着有时我会选择与我要运行的数据非常不同的示例。

611
00:48:20,315 --> 00:48:39,790
So if I have a task where, let's say I was trying to extract information from factual documents, I might actually give it examples that are from children's, what sounds like a children's story, just so that I'm like, I want you to understand the task, but I don't want you to latch on too much to the words that I use, or the very specific format.
所以如果我有一个任务，比如说我想从事实性文件中提取信息，我可能会给出一些听起来像是儿童故事的示例，仅仅是为了让你理解任务，但我不希望你过于依赖我使用的词语或非常具体的格式。

612
00:48:39,930 --> 00:48:47,656
I care more about you understanding the actual thing that I want you to do, which can mean, yeah, I don't end up giving,
我更关心的是你理解我想要你做的实际事情，这可能意味着，是的，我并不会给出，

613
00:48:48,917 --> 00:48:58,219
In some cases, there's some cases where this isn't true, but if you want more flexibility and diversity, you're going to use illustrative examples rather than concrete ones.
在某些情况下，有些情况并非如此，但如果你想要更多的灵活性和多样性，你会使用说明性的示例而不是具体的示例。

614
00:48:58,459 --> 00:49:01,399
You're probably never going to put words in the model's mouth.
你可能永远不会为模型灌输话语。

615
00:49:01,619 --> 00:49:03,580
I haven't liked that in a long time, though.
不过，我已经很久不喜欢这样了。

616
00:49:03,620 --> 00:49:08,420
I don't do a few short examples involving the model having done a thing.
我不会做几个简短的示例，涉及模型完成某事。

617
00:49:08,960 --> 00:49:15,241
I think that intuition actually also comes from pre-training in a way that doesn't feel like it rings true of our LHF models.
我认为这种直觉实际上也来自于预训练，以一种听起来不符合我们LHF模型的方式。

618
00:49:16,182 --> 00:49:18,102
So yeah, I think those are differences.
所以是的，我认为这些是区别。

619
00:49:18,502 --> 00:49:30,894
One thing I'd add, a lot of times if you're prompting, like if I'm writing prompts to use on cloud.ai, it's like I'm iterating until I get it right one time, and then it's out the window, I'm good, I did it.
我想补充一点，很多时候如果你在提示，比如如果我写提示以在cloud.ai上使用，我就是在迭代，直到我有一次做对为止，然后就扔掉，我很好，我做到了。

620
00:49:31,514 --> 00:49:37,940
Whereas most enterprise prompts, it's like you're going to go use this thing a million times, or 10 million times, or 100 million times, or something like that.
而大多数企业提示，就像你可能会用这个东西一百万次，或者一千万次，或者一亿次，之类的。

621
00:49:40,937 --> 00:49:49,871
the care and thought you put in is very much testing against the whole range of things, ways this could be used and the range of input data.
你投入的关心和思考实际上是针对这可能被使用的整个范围和输入数据范围进行测试。

622
00:49:50,291 --> 00:49:55,399
Whereas a lot of my time is thinking about one specific thing I want the model to get done right now.
而我大部分时间在思考的是我希望模型现在完成的一个特定事情。

623
00:49:56,360 --> 00:50:05,165
And it's a pretty big difference in like how I approach prompting between like if I just want to get it done this one time, right, versus if I want to like build a system that gets it right a million times.
在我想要一次性完成的事情与我想要构建一个能正确完成一百万次的系统之间，这是一种相当大的不同。

624
00:50:06,146 --> 00:50:06,986
Yeah, definitely.
是的，绝对如此。

625
00:50:07,147 --> 00:50:12,390
In the chat setting, you have the ability to keep the human in the loop, right, and just keep going back and forth.
在聊天环境中，你有能力保持人类在环中，对吧，可以不断往返。

626
00:50:12,550 --> 00:50:20,475
Whereas, yeah, when you're writing for a prompt to, you know, power a chatbot system, it has to cover the whole spectrum of what it could possibly encounter.
而且，是的，当你为提示写作，以便让聊天机器人系统运行时，它必须涵盖它可能遇到的整个范围。

627
00:50:20,495 --> 00:50:23,997
It's a lot lower stakes when you are on Cloud.ai and you're writing.
当你在Cloud.ai上写作时，风险要低得多。

628
00:50:24,861 --> 00:50:27,982
tell it that it got it wrong, or you can even edit your message and try again.
告诉它它错了，或者你甚至可以编辑你的消息再试一次。

629
00:50:28,642 --> 00:50:40,084
But if you're designing for the delightfully discontent user, then, divinely discontent user, then you can't ask them to do anything more than the minimum.
但如果你是为令人愉快的不满用户设计的，那么，你不能要求他们做任何超过最低限度的事情。

630
00:50:40,425 --> 00:50:43,105
But good prompts, I would say, are still good across both those things.
但我会说好的提示在这两种情况下仍然是好的。

631
