Sometimes I'll do it in collaboration with Claude to get it to figure out what the concept is.
有时我会和Claude合作，帮助我理解这个概念。

912
01:14:02,811 --> 01:14:06,333
Just because I'm trying to convey to it what's in my head.
只是因为我想要向它传达我脑海中的想法。

913
01:14:07,374 --> 01:14:13,038
And right now the models aren't trying to do that with us unless you kind of prompt them to do so.
而现在模型并不会主动这样做，除非你引导它们去做。

914
01:14:13,058 --> 01:14:18,662
And so in the future it might just be that they can elicit that from us rather than us having to do it for them.
所以未来可能只是它们能从我们这里引导出这些，而不是我们必须为它们去做。

915
01:14:22,925 --> 01:14:29,610
But I think another thing that's kind of interesting, this is like a, people have sometimes asked me like, oh, where is like philosophy relevant to prompting?
但我觉得还有另一件有趣的事情，人们有时会问我，哲学与提示有什么关系。

916
01:14:30,310 --> 01:14:32,412
And I actually think it's like very useful in a sense.
我实际上认为这在某种意义上是非常有用的。

917
01:14:32,592 --> 01:14:40,938
So like a lot of, there is like a style of philosophy writing, and this is at least how I was taught how to write philosophy, where the idea is that in order to
就像有一种哲学写作风格，这至少是我学习哲学写作的方式，理念是为了

918
01:14:42,339 --> 01:14:50,667
I think it's like an anti-bullshit device in philosophy basically, which is that your papers and what you write should be legible to a kind of educated layperson.
我认为这在哲学中是一个反对废话的工具，也就是说，你的论文和所写的东西应该对一种受过教育的外行人是可理解的。

919
01:14:51,087 --> 01:14:55,070
Someone just finds your paper, they pick it up and they start reading it and they can understand everything.
有人随便找到你的论文，拿起来开始阅读，他们能理解一切。

920
01:14:55,914 --> 01:15:03,856
Not everyone achieves this, but that's kind of the goal of the discipline, I guess, or at least this is at least what we kind of teach people.
并不是每个人都能做到这一点，但这大概是这个学科的目标，或者至少这是我们教给人们的。

921
01:15:05,817 --> 01:15:13,639
And so I'm really used to this idea of when I'm writing, thinking about the kind of educated layperson who they're really smart, but they don't know anything about this topic.
所以我非常习惯在写作时考虑到这样一个受过教育的外行人，他们非常聪明，但对这个主题一无所知。

922
01:15:14,159 --> 01:15:16,480
And that was just like years and years of writing text.
这就是多年来写作的经验。

923
01:15:17,020 --> 01:15:20,541
of that form, and I think it was just really good for prompting, because I was like, oh, I'm used to this.
这种形式的写作，我觉得这对提示非常有帮助，因为我想，哦，我对此很熟悉。

924
01:15:20,841 --> 01:15:28,682
I have an educated layperson who doesn't know anything about the topic, and what I need to do is I need to take extremely complex ideas, and I need to make them understand it.
我有一个对这个主题一无所知的受过教育的外行人，而我需要做的就是将极其复杂的想法转化为他们能够理解的内容。

925
01:15:28,702 --> 01:15:30,563
I don't talk down to them.
我不以高人一等的态度对待他们。

926
01:15:30,663 --> 01:15:43,405
I'm not inaccurate, but I need to phrase things in such a way that it's extremely clear to them what I mean, and prompting felt very similar, and actually, the training techniques we use are fascinating, or the things that you said where you say to a person,
我没有不准确，但我需要以一种极其清晰的方式表达我的意思，而提示给我的感觉非常相似，实际上，我们使用的训练技巧很有趣，或者你所说的，当你对一个人说，

927
01:15:44,225 --> 01:15:45,826
just take that thing you said and write it down.
只需把你刚才说的那句话写下来。

928
01:15:45,846 --> 01:15:47,546
I used to say that to students all the time.
我以前常常对学生这么说。

929
01:15:47,906 --> 01:15:50,507
They'd write a paper and I was like, I don't quite get what you're saying here.
他们写一篇论文，我就会说，我不太明白你在说什么。

930
01:15:50,547 --> 01:15:52,328
Can you just explain your argument to me?
你能给我解释一下你的论点吗？

931
01:15:52,728 --> 01:15:57,310
They would give me an incredibly cogent argument and then I'd be like, can you just take that and write it down?
他们会给我一个非常清晰的论点，然后我就会说，你能把这个写下来吗？

932
01:15:58,330 --> 01:16:00,611
And then if they did, that was often a great essay.
如果他们这样做，那通常会是篇好论文。

933
01:16:01,252 --> 01:16:04,673
So it's really interesting that there's at least that similarity of just like,
所以有趣的是，至少有这种相似性，就像是，

934
01:16:05,273 --> 01:16:16,320
taking things that are in your brain, analyzing them enough to feel like you fully understand them and could take any person off the street who's like a reasonable person and just like externalize your brain into them.
从你的脑海中提取东西，分析得足够清楚，以至于你感觉自己完全理解了，并且能够把任何一个街上的人，像一个理智的人一样，外化到他们身上。

935
01:16:16,440 --> 01:16:18,121
And I feel like that's like the core of prompting.
我觉得这就是提示的核心。

936
01:16:19,202 --> 01:16:23,605
That might be the best summary of how to prompt well I've ever heard.
这可能是我听过的关于如何好好提示的最佳总结。

937
01:16:23,945 --> 01:16:25,085
In fact, I'm pretty sure it is.
实际上，我几乎可以肯定这是。

938
01:16:26,086 --> 01:16:27,167
Externalize your brain.
外化你的思维。

939
01:16:27,267 --> 01:16:28,067
And then we'll cut to them.
然后我们会切换到他们。

940
01:16:29,128 --> 01:16:32,970
In education, in the thing is a really good way to describe the thing.
在教育中，描述这个事情的方式真的很好。

941
01:16:33,071 --> 01:16:33,431
That was good.
这很好。

942
01:16:33,993 --> 01:16:37,137
That's, I think, a great way to wrap this conversation.
我觉得这是结束这个对话的一个优秀方式。

943
01:16:37,157 --> 01:16:39,100
Thank you guys, this was great.
谢谢大家，这真是太棒了。

